   1.00   1e-02       0.465381      0.116345      9.8696       2.4674       4.605
   1.00   1e-03       0.206836      0.051709      9.8696       2.4674       6.908
   1.00   1e-04       0.116345      0.029086      9.8696       2.4674       9.210
   2.00   1e-02       0.001007      0.000252      9.8696       2.4674      99.000
   2.00   1e-03       0.000010      0.000002      9.8696       2.4674     999.000
   2.00   1e-04       0.000000      0.000000      9.8696       2.4674    9999.000
  exact free-Laplacian targets: clamped (D-D) lam1*Y^2 = pi^2 = 9.8696;
  free (N-D) lam1*Y^2 = pi^2/4 = 2.4674.
  reading:
   beta < 1 (Y finite): lam_1 converges to TWO DIFFERENT finite
     limits - e.g. beta = 0.5: clamped -> pi^2/4 = 2.4674, free ->
     pi^2/16 = 0.6169 (Y -> 2): the locus is a regular endpoint at
     FINITE record distance; the continuum theory is undetermined
     until a boundary record decides: NAKED.
   beta >= 1 (Y infinite): both towers collapse onto the SAME free
     record geometry (lam1*Y^2 pinned at pi^2 and pi^2/4 while
     lam_1 -> 0): the clamped/free label rides at infinite record
     distance, invisible to every finite sealed diamond: CENSORED.
   beta = 1 is marginal (Y ~ ln(1/x_min), the P12 log threshold);
   beta = 2 censors at power rate (Y ~ 1/x_min): extremal-fast.

== (iii) extremal coldness: the smooth period tracks 1/N'(0) ==
    a     kappa_a = a/(1+a)   beta tested        tip exponent
   0.50       0.3333        2pi/kappa_a         0.9926
   0.50       0.3333        2pi (naive)         2.9779
   0.25       0.2000        2pi/kappa_a         0.9853
   0.25       0.2000        2pi (naive)         4.9269
  -> the tip is smooth (exponent 1) only at beta = 2pi/N'(0):
     T = N'(0)/2pi.  As a -> 0 (extremal limit, N ~ x^2) the
     smooth period diverges: EXTREMAL RECORD HORIZONS ARE COLD
     (T -> 0); the Euclidean tip degenerates to a cusp that no
     finite period closes.
